# HVAC Energy Optimizer — Physics-Informed ML for HVAC Systems

By **Sabbir Hossain** · research-grade Python project · [GitHub](https://github.com/shuvo1901/Sabbir-Hossain)

Forecast cooling load with gradient boosting, then cut energy with
supply-air-temperature reset, chiller sequencing, and look-ahead pre-cooling —
all evaluated with **ASHRAE Guideline 14** metrics.

```
synthetic BMS data → 2R2C thermal model → GBM forecaster → supervisory optimization → kWh savings
```

## Results (synthetic 60-day demo)

| Strategy | Energy | Saving vs baseline |
|---|---|---|
| Baseline (fixed SAT 13 °C) | reference | — |
| SAT reset 12→16 °C | lower | typically 6–14%* |
| Chiller sequencing | lower | typically 3–8%* |
| MPC pre-cooling | lower | scenario-dependent |

\* ranges depend on climate/seed; run `examples/run_demo.py` for your exact numbers.
Forecast quality target: hourly **CVRMSE ≤ 30%**, **|NMBE| ≤ 10%** (ASHRAE G14).

## Quickstart

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
pip install -e .

# 1. generate data
python scripts/generate_synthetic_data.py --days 365 --out data/synthetic_hvac.csv

# 2. train + evaluate forecaster
python scripts/train_forecaster.py --csv data/synthetic_hvac.csv --model gbm

# 3. compare controls
python scripts/evaluate_controls.py --csv data/synthetic_hvac.csv

# 4. full demo + figures
python examples/run_demo.py --days 60 --model gbm

# 5. tests
pytest -q
```

Open `notebooks/01_hvac_energy_study.ipynb` for the full study.

## Layout

```
src/hvac_optimizer/   psychrometrics, thermal_model, forecasting, optimization, mpc, evaluation, synthetic
scripts/              generate_synthetic_data, train_forecaster, evaluate_controls
examples/run_demo.py  end-to-end pipeline (metrics.json + demo.png)
tests/                pytest suite
docs/METHODOLOGY.md   equations, assumptions, references
notebooks/            runnable study
```

## Physics notes

- Psychrometrics per ASHRAE Fundamentals (Tetens/Magnus, SI).
- 2R2C zone + PLR/OAT-dependent chiller COP — see `docs/METHODOLOGY.md`.
- Synthetic climate is Dhaka-like (hot-humid); swap in measured BMS CSV for publication.

